        "original_id": "fbb4e3a8-a563-5382-98ba-cafa90adb2d6",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Works: Iphigénie en Tauride; Der Barbier von Bagdad\n- People: Christoph Willibald Gluck; Peter Cornelius; Nicolas-François Guillard\n- Concepts: opera; libretto; One Thousand and One Nights\n\nFacts:\n- In 1779, Christoph Willibald Gluck composed Iphigénie en Tauride, an opera in four acts.\n- Nicolas-François Guillard’s first libretto was Iphigénie en Tauride, which became his most famous work.\n- Christoph Willibald Gluck initially rejected Nicolas-François Guillard’s libretto for Iphigénie en Tauride before later setting it.\n- Der Barbier von Bagdad is a comic opera in two acts by Peter Cornelius.\n- Peter Cornelius wrote the German libretto for Der Barbier von Bagdad himself.\n- Der Barbier von Bagdad is based on The Tale of the Tailor and The Barber’s Stories of his Six Brothers in One Thousand and One Nights.",
